          Greenworks is the better pick for most buyers: its brushless corded models carry a 10-year motor warranty that Ryobi does not match, it sells across Amazon, Lowe's, Walmart and its own store, and its 60V and 80V cordless units reach 3,000 PSI. Ryobi is the better pick if you already own 40V batteries, want to walk into a Home Depot for returns and accessories, or want a tidy, well-designed corded machine like the RY142500 at a fair price.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            On corded electric pressure washers, the two brands are closer than the box art suggests. Both top out at 3,000 PSI in the mainstream lineup, both deliver roughly 1.1 to 1.2 gallons per minute at full pressure, and both use the same quarter-inch quick-connect nozzle tips that most of the industry shares. A 2,500 PSI Ryobi and a 2,700 PSI Greenworks will clean a deck or a stretch of siding at very similar speed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Greenworks wins on breadth and warranty. It sells dozens of corded models from about $110 to about $800, it puts a 10-year motor warranty on its TruBrushless machines on top of the three-year tool warranty, and its cordless units on the 60V and 80V platforms reach 3,000 PSI. You can buy Greenworks from its own site, Amazon, Lowe's, Walmart and Best Buy, which makes price shopping and replacement parts easy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi wins on ecosystem and convenience. It is a Home Depot brand, so every model, accessory and surface cleaner is on the same shelf, and returns are a drive away. If you already own Ryobi 40V tools, the 40V HP Whisper Series pressure washer runs on batteries you have and carries a five-year warranty. Ryobi's corded brushless machines are well thought out, with big wheels, long hoses and good onboard storage.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only want the bottom line on what to buy, our tested shortlist is in <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/">the best electric pressure washers guide</a>, which ranks Ryobi and Greenworks models against Karcher, Sun Joe, Westinghouse and DeWalt. If you are open to gas too, the wider field is in <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">our overall best pressure washer picks</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compare at full pressure, not at the headline.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Both brands print a maximum PSI, and Greenworks also prints a flow figure measured at 100 PSI. At working pressure, nearly every corded model from either brand moves 1.1 to 1.2 GPM. Buy on motor type, warranty and ergonomics, not on the bigger number.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lineups" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Current Lineups
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi's corded range at Home Depot is short and easy to read. The RY142012 is a 2,100 PSI, 1.2 GPM compact model with a 13-amp brushed motor, a built-in foamer, and full onboard storage at about 22 pounds. The RY142500 steps up to 2,500 PSI and 1.2 GPM with a 13-amp brushless induction motor, a 25-foot hose, a 35-foot GFCI cord, 12-inch wheels and a three-year warranty. At the top, the RY143011 delivers 3,000 PSI at 1.1 GPM from a water-cooled brushless motor, with a 35-foot hose and a 5-in-1 nozzle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two older Ryobi brushless models deserve a warning rather than a recommendation. The RY142300 2,300 PSI and the RY142711VNM 2,700 PSI were recalled in August 2025, along with the RY142300VNM, because the motor capacitor can overheat and burst; about 764,000 units sold between 2017 and June 2024 are covered, and the fix is a free replacement capacitor kit from Ryobi. If you own one, or you are looking at a used one, check it against the recall before you use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Greenworks' corded range is much broader. At the budget end, the 1,800 PSI 1.1 GPM and 1,900 PSI 1.2 GPM models use 13-amp universal motors and sell for roughly $110-200. The middle is crowded with 2,000 to 2,400 PSI machines. The models worth attention are the TruBrushless units: the 2,300 PSI and 2,700 PSI machines rated at 1.2 GPM at full pressure, and the 3,000 PSI GPW3000 and its Gen 2 successor. Above that sit industrial-grade 2,500 and 3,100 PSI units at 2.1 GPM for considerably more money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither brand's corded models need special wiring at the lower end, but the 3,000 PSI machines from both draw enough current that a dedicated 15- or 20-amp outlet and as little extension cord as possible make a real difference. For the motor side of that story, and why brushless or induction motors handle heat better, see <a href="/induction-vs-universal-motor-pressure-washer/">induction vs universal motor pressure washers</a>.
          </p>

