        <div id="ratings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Reading the PSI and GPM Claims
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cleaning power is pressure multiplied by flow, which the industry calls cleaning units. Pressure breaks the bond between dirt and the surface; flow carries it away. A machine with high pressure and low flow strips a narrow line quickly but takes a long time to rinse a driveway. Our explainer on <a href="/pressure-washer-psi-vs-gpm/">PSI vs GPM</a> covers how to weigh the two for your jobs, but the short version is that flow is the number electric machines struggle with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Greenworks prints two flow numbers on many of its corded machines, and the bigger one is the one on the box. The GPW3000 is advertised as 3,000 PSI and 2.0 GPM, but Greenworks' own specification line says 1.1 GPM at 3,000 PSI and 2.0 GPM at 100 PSI. The 2,300 and 2,700 PSI Gen 2 models similarly list 2.3 GPM at 100 PSI and 1.2 GPM at full pressure. At 100 PSI you are rinsing, not pressure washing, so the full-pressure figure is the honest comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the math at working pressure and the brands line up closely. The Ryobi RY142500 gives about 3,000 cleaning units, the Ryobi RY143011 about 3,300, the Greenworks 2,700 PSI model about 3,240, and the GPW3000 about 3,300. In other words, the top corded machines from both brands are roughly equivalent, and both are well short of a gas machine making 3,000 PSI at 2.5 GPM. Anyone hoping a 3,000 PSI electric will match a gas washer on a big driveway should adjust expectations.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peak PSI is also measured at the pump in most cases, not at the nozzle. Some brands publish ratings certified to the Pressure Washer Manufacturers Association standard, which measures under defined conditions; Greenworks marks several of its models as PWMA certified, including its 60V 3,000 PSI unit. That is worth a small preference when two machines look identical on paper, because it means the number was measured rather than estimated.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is simple. For siding, fences, decks, patio furniture and cars, any brushless model from either brand at 2,300 PSI or above is plenty. For concrete, both brands' 2,500 to 3,000 PSI corded models will work with a surface cleaner, but they will be slow on a two-car driveway. Pick on motor, warranty and handling, and do not pay extra for a flow figure measured at 100 PSI.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motors-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Motors and Warranty
          </h2>

          <p>
            The motor is the biggest predictor of how long an electric pressure washer lasts. Cheap machines use universal motors with carbon brushes, which are loud, run hot, and wear out; brushless and induction motors run cooler and quieter and last far longer. Ryobi describes the RY142500 as a brushless induction motor and the RY143011 as a water-cooled brushless motor; its compact RY142012 is brushed. Greenworks uses universal motors on its cheapest models and its TruBrushless motors on the mid and upper range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty is where Greenworks pulls clearly ahead. Its TruBrushless corded models carry a three-year limited tool warranty plus a 10-year warranty on the brushless motor, and its 60V and 80V 3,000 PSI cordless units carry four years on the tool and 10 years on the motor. Ryobi's corded pressure washers carry a three-year warranty, with no separate long motor term. Its 40V HP pressure washer is the exception, at five years, in line with the rest of Ryobi's 40V tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the motor warranty for what it is. Pumps, unloader valves, hoses and triggers fail more often than brushless motors do, and those parts fall under the shorter tool warranty on both brands. A 10-year motor warranty is still a meaningful signal of confidence, and it covers the single most expensive part to replace, but it does not mean the whole machine is covered for a decade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands' brushless machines include an automatic total-stop system that shuts the motor off when you release the trigger, which reduces heat in the pump. Even so, the most common cause of early pump failure on either brand is running dry or leaving the trigger released with the motor forced on. Connect the water, purge air from the hose, and start the motor only after water flows freely from the wand.
          </p>

          <p>
            If longevity is the reason you are buying, choose a brushless model from either brand, not the budget universal-motor units. Between brushless models, Greenworks' longer motor warranty is the tiebreaker unless you place real value on Ryobi's return convenience at Home Depot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cordless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cordless: Ryobi 40V vs Greenworks 60V and 80V
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi's main battery pressure washer is the 40V HP Brushless Whisper Series, model RY40306BTL, rated at 2,000 PSI and 1.2 GPM with three power settings from 1,000 to 2,000 PSI. It takes two 40V batteries and Ryobi quotes up to 45 minutes of runtime on two 6Ah packs. It ships with a 10-foot siphon hose and self-priming adapter, so it can draw from a bucket or rain barrel, and it carries Ryobi's five-year warranty. A smaller 1,500 PSI 40V model is also sold. Ryobi's 18V ONE+ cleaners are low-pressure tools, not pressure washers in the usual sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Greenworks goes further on power. Its 60V and 80V 3,000 PSI cordless washers are rated at up to 3,000 PSI and 2.0 GPM at maximum power, with the 60V model also offering an ECO mode at 2,200 PSI and 1.0 GPM to stretch runtime. The 60V model is PWMA certified, and both carry four-year tool and 10-year motor warranties. There are also hybrid 60V models that run from either the wall or batteries, including an 1,800 PSI unit that drops to 1,550 PSI on battery power, and a 3,000 PSI hybrid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery cost dominates the decision. The bare tools are priced like mid-range corded machines, but two large batteries and a charger can cost as much again. If you already own Ryobi 40V or Greenworks 60V or 80V tools, the pressure washer is a reasonable add-on. If you own neither, a corded brushless washer gives you more sustained cleaning for far less money, and our <a href="/cordless-vs-corded-pressure-washer/">cordless vs corded pressure washer</a> comparison explains the trade in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runtime at full pressure is the real limit on any battery washer. Treat battery models as tools for boats, cars, outdoor furniture, and fences far from an outlet, not for whole-house siding or driveway jobs. On those, a corded machine running all afternoon will finish long before the batteries do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accessories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hose Fittings, Nozzles and Surface Cleaners
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the business end, both brands use standard quarter-inch quick-connect nozzle tips, so aftermarket 0, 15, 25 and 40 degree tips, turbo nozzles and foam cannons with a quarter-inch plug fit either brand's wand. Ryobi's newer machines include a 5-in-1 quick-change nozzle plus a turbo nozzle on the RY143011, and three quarter-inch tips on the RY142500. Greenworks typically includes five nozzles on its brushless models, including a turbo, and sells universal replacement tips and turbo nozzles rated to 3,100 or 4,500 PSI.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose-end fittings are less uniform. Many Greenworks machines and replacement hoses use the common M22-14mm thread, which is the easiest to source. Some Ryobi models use brand-specific hose connections that need an adapter for a standard M22 hose. Before buying a longer aftermarket hose for either brand, check the manual or the fitting on your machine, and match the hose's pressure rating to your washer's maximum PSI.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface cleaners turn a slow concrete job into a manageable one, and both brands sell their own. Ryobi offers 12-inch surface cleaners, including a model with casters, often bundled with the RY142500 and RY143011 at Home Depot. Greenworks sells universal 11-, 12- and 15-inch rotating surface cleaners, plus a 15-inch cleaner with a wand extension. On 1.1 to 1.2 GPM electric machines, an 11 to 12 inch cleaner is the right size; a 15-inch head spreads too little water and slows down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Any surface cleaner with a quarter-inch quick-connect plug and a pressure rating at or above your machine's maximum will fit either brand. For tested picks across sizes and price points, see <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">the best surface cleaners for pressure washers</a>, which also explains why low-flow electric washers need smaller heads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parts-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Parts, Where to Buy, and Who Each Brand Suits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where you buy matters more than people expect, because it determines how easy it is to return a dud or replace a broken trigger. Ryobi tools are sold at Home Depot and Ryobi's Direct Tools Factory Outlet, so returns, accessories, and some replacement parts are available in person. Ryobi also runs its own parts and service network for anything outside the store's return window. For a broader read on how the brand holds up across its tool range, see <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">our Ryobi tools review</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Greenworks is spread across more retailers, including its own store, Amazon, Lowe's, Walmart and Best Buy, which helps on price and makes it easy to find a sale. The trade-off is that returns depend on where you bought it, and parts usually come by mail from Greenworks or a third-party seller rather than from a store shelf. Greenworks' own store sells replacement hoses, wands and gun kits, nozzles and surface cleaners directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Greenworks if you want the most choice, the longest motor warranty, the strongest cordless option, and the flexibility to shop around. The TruBrushless 2,300 or 2,700 PSI corded models are the sweet spot for most homes, and the 60V or 80V 3,000 PSI units make sense if you already own those batteries. Avoid the cheapest universal-motor models unless you only clean a car and a patio set a few times a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Ryobi if you already own 40V batteries, value walking into a Home Depot for returns and accessories, or want a well-designed corded machine with large wheels and long hoses. The RY142500 is the pick of the current corded lineup for most homes, and the RY143011 for those who want 3,000 PSI. Before buying any used Ryobi brushless washer, check its model number against the 2025 recall. To see how these brands stack up against the rest of the field, start with <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/">our tested electric pressure washer rankings</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Greenworks for most buyers, Ryobi for Ryobi owners.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Greenworks' brushless models match Ryobi on real output and beat it on motor warranty and retail choice. Ryobi earns the sale when you already own 40V batteries or want Home Depot's in-store convenience.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing flow figures measured at 100 PSI</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Greenworks lists the GPW3000 as 2.0 GPM, but that figure is at 100 PSI; at 3,000 PSI it is 1.1 GPM. Compare every machine at full working pressure, where Ryobi and Greenworks brushless models land within a few hundred cleaning units of each other, and do not pay extra for the inflated number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a universal-motor model to save fifty dollars</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Budget models from both brands use brushed or universal motors that run hot, get loud and wear out. Spending the extra for a brushless machine usually buys several more years of service, and on Greenworks it also buys a 10-year motor warranty. For anything beyond occasional car washing, it is money well spent.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Ryobi recall on used machines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The RY142300, RY142300VNM and RY142711VNM were recalled in August 2025 because the capacitor can overheat and burst. Roughly 764,000 units were sold before June 2024, so plenty circulate secondhand. Check the model number, and get the free repair kit from Ryobi before using one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a cordless washer without counting battery cost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bare cordless tools look cheap next to corded machines, but two large batteries and a charger can double the price. Unless you already own Ryobi 40V or Greenworks 60V or 80V batteries, a corded brushless washer gives more sustained cleaning for much less money and never runs flat mid-job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an oversized surface cleaner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 15-inch surface cleaner spreads 1.1 to 1.2 GPM too thin and cleans slower than a smaller head. On electric machines from either brand, an 11 to 12 inch cleaner rated above your maximum PSI with a quarter-inch quick-connect plug is the right match and fits both brands' wands.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi or Greenworks better for pressure washers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Greenworks is better for most buyers because its brushless models match Ryobi's real output, carry a 10-year motor warranty, and are sold at more retailers. Ryobi is better if you already own its 40V batteries or want Home Depot's in-store returns and accessories. At equal pressure and flow, cleaning speed is very similar between the two brands.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Ryobi electric pressure washers brushless?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some are. The RY142500 uses a 13-amp brushless induction motor and the RY143011 uses a water-cooled brushless motor, while the compact RY142012 uses a brushed motor. The 40V HP Whisper Series cordless model is brushless. Older brushless models, the RY142300 and RY142711VNM, were recalled in 2025 and should be checked before use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Greenworks 3000 PSI really 2.0 GPM?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only at very low pressure. Greenworks' own listing for the corded 3,000 PSI models says 1.1 GPM at 3,000 PSI and 2.0 GPM at 100 PSI. At working pressure it makes about 3,300 cleaning units, roughly the same as Ryobi's 3,000 PSI RY143011. It cleans well, but it is not a match for gas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty do Ryobi and Greenworks pressure washers have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ryobi corded pressure washers carry a three-year warranty, and its 40V HP cordless model carries five years. Greenworks gives three years on the tool plus 10 years on the motor for TruBrushless corded models, and four years on the tool plus 10 years on the motor for its 60V and 80V 3,000 PSI cordless washers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Greenworks accessories fit Ryobi pressure washers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Nozzle-end accessories generally do, because both brands use quarter-inch quick-connect tips, so surface cleaners, turbo nozzles and foam cannons with that plug fit either wand. Hose fittings are less consistent. Many Greenworks hoses use M22-14mm threads, while some Ryobi machines need an adapter. Check the fitting before you buy a replacement hose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a Ryobi or Greenworks electric pressure washer clean concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with patience and a surface cleaner. The 2,500 to 3,000 PSI corded models from both brands remove dirt and algae from concrete, but at 1.1 to 1.2 GPM a full driveway takes considerably longer than with gas. Use an 11 to 12 inch surface cleaner and a 15-degree or turbo nozzle for edges and stains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better cordless, Ryobi 40V or Greenworks 60V?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Greenworks' 60V and 80V 3,000 PSI models are more powerful than Ryobi's 2,000 PSI 40V HP Whisper Series, and carry a 10-year motor warranty. Ryobi's model is quieter-focused, offers three pressure settings, and runs up to 45 minutes on two 6Ah batteries. If you already own one platform's batteries, that usually decides it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

